# FIN physical-claim ladder

Date: 2026-09-23. Proposed campaign policy; not a change to AGENTS.md.

## 1. Five levels, five different obligations

| Level | Meaning | Required evidence |
|---|---|---|
| M — Mathematical theorem | Proposition about a specified model | Assumptions, quantifiers, proof/certificate, dependencies and controls |
| PM — Physical modelling assumption | Added interpretation, interaction, dynamics, units or protocol | Motivation, alternatives, identifiable content and a failure test |
| PB — Physical bridge theorem | Proved conditional map to operationally defined physical quantities | Domain/codomain, observables, units, errors and limit/causal hypotheses |
| PP — Physical prediction | Observable consequence of M + PM + PB | Fixed parameters, uncertainty, measurable statistic and discriminating alternatives |
| EV — Empirical validation | Comparison with independent observations | Calibration, raw events, controls, held-out scoring and reproducibility |

Every PP must exhibit `M -> PM -> PB -> PP`. Logically, M and PM are joint
premises of PB: **M does not prove PM**. An unproved bridge is PB_CANDIDATE,
not PB. A simulation is mathematical/numerical evidence or a synthetic protocol
check, not EV. Empirical agreement supports a scoped claim, not absolute ontology.

A bridge cannot make an arbitrary object physically real by definition. Its
target is an operational measurement model whose link to an apparatus remains
testable. A theorem about an assumed Hamiltonian does not derive that Hamiltonian.

## 2. Independent status axes

Record separately:

- level: M / PM / PB / PP / EV;
- proof status: proposed / analytic / interval-assisted / numerical / refuted;
- integration: package candidate / reviewed / accepted by human;
- source: strict-derived / conditional-minimal / conditional-phenomenological /
  empirical-input / speculative;
- domain: states, parameters, graph/refinement, preparation and observation class;
- independence: same-code replay / independent arithmetic / independent
  implementation / formal proof.

No number of PASS flags supplies a missing source or measurement premise.

